from tnsoc import *
S(10,22,"Asha asked how we know whether the whole country is growing. \"By GDP,\" said her teacher.",
 ["Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is the total value of all final goods and services produced in a country in a year. Gross National Product (GNP) adds income earned from abroad. Net National Product and per capita income are other measures.",
  "GDP is calculated by the expenditure, income and production methods. A rise in GDP means economic growth. Real GDP is adjusted for price changes and nominal GDP is not.",
  "India's economy has three sectors, and services now contribute the most to GDP. Growth should also be inclusive and sustainable, and Tamil Nadu is among the larger state economies."],
 ["GDP","GNP","per capita income","economic growth","real GDP","services sector"],
 ("What does GDP measure?",["Value of goods and services produced.","In one year.","Within the country."]),
 ("GDP counts only farm products.",["It counts all final goods and services.","Industry and services too."]),
 ["GDP = value of final output in a year.","GNP adds foreign income.","Services lead India's GDP.","Real GDP removes price effect."],
 ["m|GDP is the value of|final goods and services|only exports|only taxes|Produced in a year.","t|Services give the largest share of GDP in India.|T|Yes.","m|Per capita income is income per|person|village|state|Average.","t|GDP counts only farm products.|F|All sectors.","m|Real GDP is adjusted for|prices|population|rain|Inflation.","s|GDP stands for Gross what Product?|domestic|Gross Domestic Product."])
S(10,23,"Meena found a toy labelled 'Made in China'. \"Why are goods from everywhere?\" she asked.",
 ["Globalisation means countries are linked through trade, investment and technology. It grew after 1991 when India liberalised its economy with reforms such as lower import duties and freer private investment.",
  "International trade is the exchange of goods and services among countries, and the World Trade Organisation (WTO) sets rules. Multinational companies set up plants in many countries. Foreign direct investment (FDI) brings money and skills.",
  "Globalisation gives choice, jobs and technology, but small producers face tough competition. A balance of trade and wise policy protect local industry."],
 ["globalisation","liberalisation","WTO","multinational company","FDI","trade"],
 ("What are the benefits of globalisation?",["More choice for consumers.","Jobs and technology.","Wider markets for producers."]),
 ("India liberalised its economy in 1947.",["Reforms began in 1991.","1947 was Independence."]),
 ["Globalisation links countries.","Reforms in 1991.","WTO sets trade rules.","FDI brings investment."],
 ["m|Economic reforms in India began in|1991|1947|2000|Liberalisation.","t|WTO sets trade rules.|T|Yes.","m|FDI is foreign direct|investment|import|income|Money from abroad.","t|Globalisation means no trade.|F|More trade.","m|A multinational company works in|many countries|one village|one state|Global business.","s|What does WTO stand for: World Trade what?|organisation|Organisation."])
S(10,24,"Raju saw a family receiving free rice at a ration shop. \"Why is that?\" he asked.",
 ["Food security means that all people have enough safe and nutritious food at all times. It needs food availability, access and the ability to absorb it. Poverty, droughts and rising prices threaten it.",
  "In India the Public Distribution System (PDS) supplies food grains at low prices through ration shops, and the Food Corporation of India keeps buffer stocks. The National Food Security Act of 2013 gives subsidised grain to many families.",
  "Tamil Nadu runs a universal PDS and a noon meal scheme. Nutrition is also a part: balanced diet, protein, vitamins and minerals prevent malnutrition and stunting."],
 ["food security","PDS","buffer stock","ration shop","malnutrition","National Food Security Act"],
 ("How does the PDS help the poor?",["It gives grain at low prices.","Through ration shops.","It protects against hunger."]),
 ("Food security means only having rice.",["It needs enough nutritious food for all.","Variety and nutrition count."]),
 ["Food security: access to enough food.","PDS ration shops.","FCI buffer stock.","Noon meals help nutrition."],
 ["m|PDS stands for Public Distribution|System|Service|Scheme|Ration supply.","t|The FCI keeps buffer stocks.|T|Yes.","m|Poor diet leads to|malnutrition|taxes|speed|Lack of nutrition.","t|Food security needs only grain.|F|Nutrition too.","m|The noon meal scheme helps|children|banks|roads|Nutrition.","s|What is the shop for subsidised grain called?|ration shop|PDS."])
S(10,25,"Meena heard her father mention paying tax. \"Why do we pay tax?\" she asked.",
 ["A government earns revenue and spends it on public services. Its revenue comes from taxes and non-tax sources such as fees and profits from public enterprises. Taxes are direct, like income tax paid by the person, and indirect, like GST added to prices.",
  "Goods and Services Tax (GST) replaced many indirect taxes in 2017. The budget is the yearly statement of the government's income and expenditure. A deficit occurs when spending is more than income.",
  "Tax money builds roads, schools, hospitals and defence, and funds welfare schemes. Paying tax honestly is a duty of citizens."],
 ["revenue","direct tax","indirect tax","GST","budget","deficit"],
 ("How does tax help the people?",["It builds roads and schools.","It pays for hospitals and defence.","It funds welfare."]),
 ("Income tax is an indirect tax.",["It is a direct tax.","GST is indirect."]),
 ["Government revenue: taxes and fees.","Direct and indirect taxes.","GST from 2017.","Budget shows income and spending."],
 ["m|GST is an|indirect tax|direct tax|loan|Added on goods.","t|Income tax is a direct tax.|T|Yes.","m|A budget is a yearly|income and spending plan|tax|loan|Statement.","t|Taxes pay for roads and schools.|T|Yes.","m|Spending above income is a|deficit|surplus|profit|Gap.","s|GST was introduced in which year?|2017|Replaced many taxes."])
S(10,26,"Raju saw rows of power looms in Erode. \"Why are the same industries in the same place?\" he asked.",
 ["An industrial cluster is a group of firms of the same industry in one area that share skills, suppliers and markets. Tamil Nadu has many famous clusters.",
  "Examples are Tiruppur for knitwear, Coimbatore for textiles and pumps, Sivakasi for fireworks and printing, Karur for home textiles, Salem for steel and Erode for textiles, and Ambattur and Hosur for engineering.",
  "Clusters create jobs, make goods cheaper and spread skills, but they need good roads, power, water and care for the environment. Small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) form the backbone of the clusters."],
 ["industrial cluster","Tiruppur","Sivakasi","Coimbatore","MSME","knitwear"],
 ("Why do industries form clusters?",["They share skills and suppliers.","Costs fall.","Buyers know where to find them."]),
 ("Sivakasi is famous for knitwear.",["Tiruppur is famous for knitwear.","Sivakasi is for fireworks and printing."]),
 ["Cluster: firms of one industry together.","Tiruppur: knitwear.","Sivakasi: fireworks.","MSMEs form the base."],
 ["m|Tiruppur is famous for|knitwear|fireworks|steel|Garments.","t|Sivakasi makes fireworks.|T|Yes.","m|A group of similar firms in one area is a|cluster|bank|court|Shared strengths.","t|Clusters create jobs.|T|Yes.","m|MSME stands for Micro, Small and Medium|Enterprises|Exports|Estates|Small business.","s|Which city is known for pumps and textiles?|coimbatore|Manchester of South India."])
